1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,700
DEV: AI wrote the feature and tests.

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:05,500
GUIDE: And you shipped it.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,600
DEV: Then prod broke.

4
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:10,000
GUIDE: Three endpoints you never touched.

5
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:12,000
DEV: And nothing failed.

6
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:15,000
GUIDE: AI only imagined a few users.

7
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:16,500
DEV: Five, maybe.

8
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:20,000
DEV: So who covers the rest?

9
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:23,400
GUIDE: Keploy reads the traffic you already have.

10
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:24,800
DEV: And?

11
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:27,000
GUIDE: And works out everyone else.

12
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:30,200
GUIDE: Keploy sits between you and your dependencies.

13
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:32,500
DEV: Recording all of it.

14
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,600
GUIDE: Then Keploy writes the ordinary cases.

15
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:38,000
DEV: And the awkward ones.

16
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:41,000
GUIDE: Keploy runs every one again.

17
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:42,500
DEV: No live database.

18
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:45,600
GUIDE: Keploy returns passes and failures together.

19
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:49,000
DEV: So nothing slips through quietly.

20
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:50,600
DEV: Found here.

21
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:52,000
GUIDE: Not in production.

